\id NAM Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Nahum
\toc1 The Prophecy of Nahum.
\toc2 Nahum
\toc3 NAM
\mt1 The Prophecy of Nahum.
\is THE PROPHECIE OF NAHUM.
\ip \it NAHVM\it* borne in \it Elceſe\it* a towne in Galiley, \it prophecied shortly after the captiuitie of the tenne\it* \f - \ft Nahum prophecied about 50. yeares after Ionas, nere 135. before the deſtruction of Niniue.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Iero. Epiſt. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it tribes, againſt Niniue\it*, about fiftie yeares after Ionas preaching, and their repentance; when they became more wicked then before: foreshewing their vtter ruine and ignominie. In figure of the deſtruction of idolatrie in al nations, by Chriſt \it euangelizing and announcing peace\it* to al that wil beleue in him.
\c 1
\cd Niniue shal be deſtroyed, not able to eſcape Gods powreful wrath. 9. The Aſſirians shal not preuaile againſt Ieruſalem: 13. but themſelues shal be ſlaine.
\p
\v 1 THE \f a \fr 1.1 \ft \+it Burden\+it* ſignifieth comminatorie & penſiue prophecie of ruine and puniſhment. Allegorically \+it Nahum\+it* according to his name, comforteth the iuſt, shewing that God vvil reuenge them by deſtroying \+it Niniue the beautiful\+it* great citie, that is, the terreſtrial vvorld called \+it coſmos beautiful\+it*, and then reward his Sainctes in eternal glorie.\f* burden of Niniuie. The booke of the viſion of Nahum the Elceſeite.
\v 2 God is ielous, & our Lord reuenging, our Lord reuenging, and hauing furie: our Lord reuenging on his aduerſaries, and he is angrie with his enemies.
\v 3 Our Lord is patient, and great of ſtrength and clenſing, he wil not make innocent. Our Lords wayes in tempeſt, and whirlewind, and clowdes the duſt of his feete.
\v 4 Rebuking the ſea, and driyng it vp: and bringing al riuers to a deſert. Baſan is weakened and Carmel: & the floure of Libanus is faided.
\v 5 The mountaines were moued at him, and the hilles were deſolate: and the earth hath trembled at his preſence, and the world, and al that dwel therin.
\v 6 Before the face of his indignation who shal ſtand? and who shal reſiſt in the wrath of his furie? his indignation is powred out as fire: and the rockes are diſſolued by him.
\v 7 Our Lord is good, and ſtrenghning in the day of tribulation: and knowing them that hope in him.
\v 8 And in a floud paſſing by, he wil make a conſummation of the place therof: & darkenes shal purſew his enemies.
\v 9 VVhat thinke ye againſt our Lord? conſumation he wil make: there shal not riſe duble tribulation.
\v 10 Becauſe as thornes claſpe one an other: ſo \f b \fr 1.10 \ft The Aſſirians accounting themſelues aſſured to take and ſpoile Ieruſalem, and therupon banketting and drinking were defeated al in one night.\f* the feaſt of them that drinke together: they shal be conſumed as ſtuble ful of drienes.
\v 11 Out of thee shal come forth one that thinketh malice againſt our Lord in the minde deuiſing preuarication.
\v 12 Thus ſaith our Lord: If they shal be perfect: and ſo a great manie, ſo alſo shal they be shorne, and he shal paſſe by: I haue afflicted thee, and I wil afflict thee no more.
\v 13 And now I wil breake his rod from of thy backe, and I wil burſt thy bondes in ſunder.
\v 14 And Our Lord wil command vpon thee, there \f c \fr 1.14 \ft To Senacherib ſucceded his ſonne Aſarhaddon, but preſently after the vvhole lineage vvas deſtroyed. 4. Reg. 19.\f* shal not be ſowen of thy name any more: out of the houſe of thy God I wil deſtroy ſculptil, & molten, I wil put thy graue, becauſe thou art dishonored.
\v 15 \x - \xt Iſa. 52. Rom. 10.\x* Behold vpon the mountaines the feete of him that Euangalizeth, and preacheth peace: celebrate ô Iuda thy feſtiuities, & render thy vowes: becauſe Belial shal no more adde to paſſe through thee, he is wholly perished.
\c 2
\cd The Chaldees shal mightely inuade the Aſſirians, 5. take and ranſacke Niniue.
\p
\v 1 HE is come vp that \f a \fr 2.1 \ft Nabuchodonoſor inuading the territorie of Niniue waſted al thinges, and then aſſaulted and tooke the citie.\f* shal ſcatter before thee, that shal keepe the ſeige: behold the way, fortifie the loynes, ſtrengthen force excedingly.
\v 2 Becauſe our Lord hath rendered \f b \fr 2.2 \ft The Aſſirians became more proud and inſolent after they had ſpoiled the tvvo tribes & caried the ten into captiuitie and therfore God wil now reuenge this pride.\f* the pride of Iacob, as the pride of Iſrael: becauſe the waters haue diſſipated them, and haue marred their branches.
\v 3 The buckler of his valiants fyrie, the men of the armie in ſcarlets, the raynes of the chariot fyrie in the day of his preparation, and the driuers are brought aſleepe.
\v 4 In the wayes they are trubled, the chariots ſtrooke one agaynſt an other in the ſtreetes: their appearance are as it were lampes, as it were lightning running to and fro.
\v 5 He wil remember his valiants, they shal fal in their wayes: they shal quickly ſcale the walles therof, and a bowre shal be prepared.
\v 6 The gates of the riuers are opened, and the temple throwen downe to the ground.
\v 7 And the ſouldiar is led away captiue: and her handmayds were led away mourning as doues, murmuring in their hartes.
\v 8 And Niniue \f c \fr 2.8 \ft The people of Niniue, vvhen the wal of the citie ſhal once be broken by the enemies, wil flee away as water runneth out of a pond, or fishpoole, when the banke is broken.\f* her waters as it were a fishpoole of waters: but themſelues are fled \f d \fr 2.8 \ft Although ſome of more corege wil exhorte the fugitiues to ſtay, and fight for their citie, it wil not auaile, becauſe the moſt part vvil ſeeke to eſcape by running avvay.\f* Stand ſtand, & there is none that wil returne.
\v 9 \x - \xt Iſa. 2.\x* Spoile the ſiluer, ſpoile the gold: and there is no end of the riches of al veſſels that are to be deſired.
\v 10 She is diſſipated, and rent, and torne: and pyning hart, and diſſolution of the knees, and defect in al reynes: and the faces of them al as the blacknes of a pot.
\v 11 \f e \fr 2.11 \ft The king of Aſſirians like a furious lion gathered praye out of al countries, and brought it into Niniue as into his denne, but at laſt ſhal be ſpoyled of al.\f* VVhere is the habitation of lions, & the paſture of lions welps, to which the lion went, to goe in thither, the lions whelpe, and is there none to make them afrayd?
\v 12 The lion hath caught ſufficiently for his welpes, and hath killed for his lioneſſes: and he hath filled his dennes with praye, and his couch with rauening.
\v 13 Behold I to thee, ſaith the Lord of hoſts, and I wil burne thy chariots euen to ſmoke, and the ſword shal eate thy litle lions: and I wil deſtroy thy praye out of the land, and the voice of thy meſſengers shal be heard no more.
\c 3
\cd A deſcription of Niniues ruine, 5. made ignominious to al nations: 17. forraine ſouldiars fleing away, and al their owne terrified.
\p
\v 1 WO \x - \xt Ezec. 24. Habac. 2.\x* to thee ô citie \f a \fr 3.1 \ft Nemrod beganne with sheeding bloud to make himſelf great. \+it Gen. 10\+it*. So Ninus who built Niniue, and their ſucceſſors vvere ſtil very bloudie, and otherwiſe wicked, but at laſt after 1200 yeares, vnder Sardanapalus their Monarchie decayed much, as moſt writers both Greke & Latin teſtifie, but yet continued longer & florished againe as Doctor Ribera sheweth by the holie Scriptures, & continued in al from Ninus time til it was deſtroyed by the Chaldees, about 1440. yeares, yea & was repayred againe & was great after the relaxation of the Iewes from captiuitie. As \+it Euſebius. S. Auguſtin, S. Beda and others write\+it*.\f* of blouds, al of lying ful of tearing: ſpoile shal not depart from thee.
\v 2 The voice of the whippe, and the voice of the violence of the wheele, & of the neighing horſe, and of the feruent chariot, and of the horſman mounting.
\v 3 And of the gliſtering ſword, and of the glittering ſpeare, and of a multitude ſlaine, and of a greuous ruine: neither is there an end of corſes, and they shal fal on their bodies.
\v 4 Becauſe of the multitude of the fornications, of the harlot beautiful & gratful, and hauing ſorceries, which hath ſold nations in her fornications, & families in her ſorceries.
\v 5 \x - \xt Iſa. 47.\x* Behold I to thee, ſayth the Lord of hoſts: and I wil diſcouer thy priuie parts in thy face, and wil shew to the Gentils thy nakednes, & to kingdoms thine ignominie.
\v 6 And I wil throw vpon thee abominations, and wil vſe thee contumeliouſly, and wil put thee for an example.
\v 7 And it shal be, euerie one that shal ſee thee, shal leape backe from thee, and shal ſay: Niniue is waſted: who shal shake the head vpon thee? whence shal I ſeeke a cõforter for thee?
\v 8 Why, art thou better then \f b \fr 3.8 \ft This citie was firſt called \+it No\+it*, but being deſtroyed by the Chaldees, and reedified by K. Alexander was then called by his name. \+it S. Ierom\+it*.\f* Alexandria ful of peoples, which dwelleth in the riuers? waters round about it: whoſe riches, the ſea: waters the walles therof.
\v 9 Æthiopia the ſtrength of it, and Ægypt, & there is no end: Afrike, and the Libyans haue bene in thine ayde.
\v 10 But she alſo in tranſmigration was led into captiuitie: her litle ones were dashed in the head of al wayes, and vpon her noble ones, they haue caſt lot, and al her great men were made faſt in fetters.
\v 11 Thou alſo therfore shalt be made drunke, and shal be deſpiſed: and thou shalt ſeeke helpe of the enemie.
\v 12 Al thy munitions as figtrees with their grene figges: if they be shaken, they wil fal into the mouth of the eater.
\v 13 Behold thy people wemen in the middes of thee: to thine enimies the gates of thy land with opening shal be opened, fyre shal deuoure thy barres.
\v 14 Draw thee water for the ſiege, build thy munitions: enter into the clay, and tread, making morter hold the bricke.
\v 15 There the fyre shal eate thee: thou shalt perish by the ſword, as a bruke it shal deuoure thee: be gathered together as the bruke, be multiplied as the locuſt.
\v 16 Thou haſt made thy merchandiſes more then are the ſtarres of heauen: the bruke was ſpred, & flew away.
\v 17 Thy kepers as the locuſts: & thy litle ones as it were the locuſts of locuſts, which ſwarme on the hedges in the day of could: the ſunne aroſe, & they flew away, and their place was not knowen where they were.
\v 18 Thy paſtours ſlumbred, ô King of Aſſur, thy princes shal be buried thy people lay hid in the mountaynes, and there is none to gather them together.
\v 19 Thy deſtruction is not obſcure, thy plague is very ſore: al that haue heard the fame of thee, haue clapped the hand vpon thee: for vpon whom hath not thy malice paſſed alwayes?
